WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.767
Welcome to Module 8 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:08.525
convert Kerberos accounts to group and
delegated managed service accounts.

3
00:00:08.525 --> 00:00:11.557
This module is the
on-premises part of Gate 5,

4
00:00:11.557 --> 00:00:15.262
Conversion, with Chapter 6
of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:15.262 --> 00:00:19.671
You'll read each Kerberos
account's reviewed disposition,

6
00:00:19.671 --> 00:00:24.080
the owner's decision from Module
3, plan its move to a G M S A or,

7
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.927
on a Windows Server
2025 domain, a D M S A,

8
00:00:26.927 --> 00:00:32.622
list what stays on the Kerberos island with
an end date and, where it has S P N's,

9
00:00:32.622 --> 00:00:38.500
an S P N owner, and reconcile every managed
account with the program's end state.

10
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.400
Then you'll see the write path run
a plan only by its approved hash.

11
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.580
The domain is fictional, and the
apply changes only a fake, in memory.

12
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.480
Why does this matter?

13
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.993
Some workloads can't leave Kerberos yet.

14
00:00:54.993 --> 00:00:57.935
A traditional service
account with an S P N and a

15
00:00:57.935 --> 00:01:01.180
password that never expires
is the riskiest of them.

16
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.165
Microsoft Learn's guidance on securing
group managed service accounts says

17
00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:10.605
a G M S A gives the same workload a
password that Windows changes for it.

18
00:01:10.605 --> 00:01:16.113
And Learn's overview of delegated managed
service accounts says a D M S A, on a

19
00:01:16.113 --> 00:01:22.155
Windows Server 2025 domain, supersedes
the old account and disables its password.

20
00:01:22.155 --> 00:01:26.465
But a G M S A works only while
Active Directory is authoritative,

21
00:01:26.465 --> 00:01:30.692
which is why the program's guidance
gives every one an end date.

22
00:01:30.692 --> 00:01:32.618
So converting isn't the end.

23
00:01:32.618 --> 00:01:38.526
Every G M S A and D M S A must either
move to a workload identity, retire,

24
00:01:38.526 --> 00:01:41.857
or sit on a dated Kerberos island list,

25
00:01:41.857 --> 00:01:46.154
with an end date and, where it
has S P N's, an S P N owner.

26
00:01:46.154 --> 00:01:48.245
In this module you plan all three,

27
00:01:48.245 --> 00:01:52.160
and you see the write path
refuse a plan nobody approved.

28
00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.748
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

29
00:01:58.748 --> 00:02:01.135
which takes about sixty
minutes with the lab.

30
00:02:01.135 --> 00:02:04.660
You'll name the four dispositions
a Kerberos account can have,

31
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:05.973
and what each one needs.

32
00:02:05.973 --> 00:02:10.413
You'll explain why a D M S A can't be
used for a managed service account,

33
00:02:10.413 --> 00:02:13.723
or for an account with
unconstrained delegation.

34
00:02:13.723 --> 00:02:18.723
You'll read the plan, the island list and
the reconciliation for a fictional domain.

35
00:02:18.723 --> 00:02:21.279
And you'll show that the
write path runs only a

36
00:02:21.279 --> 00:02:23.973
plan whose hash was
approved, and never deletes.

37
00:02:23.973 --> 00:02:30.520
Before you start, finish Module 1, because
the lab reads Chapter 1's register, and

38
00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:36.563
Module 3, where the matrix sends Kerberos-only
accounts to a G M S A or a D M S A.

39
00:02:36.563 --> 00:02:41.398
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or
later with Pester 5, and a clone.

40
00:02:41.398 --> 00:02:42.670
No tenant.

41
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.245
Unit 1 is the guide's gate.

42
00:02:48.245 --> 00:02:53.663
Its Step 5 is the program's guidance:
build the target, give it two human owners,

43
00:02:53.663 --> 00:02:58.995
run old and new in parallel for thirty
days, then disable the legacy account.

44
00:02:58.995 --> 00:03:00.095
Never delete it.

45
00:03:00.095 --> 00:03:02.729
Step 2 adds the
prerequisites for a G M S A.

46
00:03:02.729 --> 00:03:07.996
Microsoft Learn's page on creating a K D S
root key says the key is created once per

47
00:03:07.996 --> 00:03:11.348
domain, and domain controllers
wait up to ten hours

48
00:03:11.348 --> 00:03:14.142
after it's created before
they allow a G M S A.

49
00:03:14.142 --> 00:03:18.771
Step 3's seventh step covers the
Active Directory side of credentials:

50
00:03:18.771 --> 00:03:24.517
Learn's guidance on securing group managed
service accounts says Windows changes the

51
00:03:24.517 --> 00:03:27.071
password every thirty days by default,

52
00:03:27.071 --> 00:03:30.982
and the interval can be set only
when the account is created.

53
00:03:30.982 --> 00:03:33.905
The rollback for Step 5 is
to re-enable the legacy

54
00:03:33.905 --> 00:03:37.120
account and revert the
application's configuration.

55
00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.837
A delegated account runs
the same thirty days:

56
00:03:39.837 --> 00:03:44.699
Learn's overview of delegated managed
service accounts recommends waiting four

57
00:03:44.699 --> 00:03:48.632
ticket lifetimes, and thirty days
covers four seven-day lifetimes,

58
00:03:48.632 --> 00:03:51.780
so the program completes
a migration only then.

59
00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.730
Unit 2 starts with the
context the code depends on.

60
00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:02.305
Every Kerberos account has
one of four dispositions,

61
00:04:02.305 --> 00:04:06.611
and it's the application owner's
decision from Module 3, D N P three,

62
00:04:06.611 --> 00:04:11.242
which this chapter reads from Chapter
3's run, never from a second list.

63
00:04:11.242 --> 00:04:17.013
Replatform: to a G M S A or a D M S A,
converted here, with the new account's name,

64
00:04:17.013 --> 00:04:21.218
the workload and its hosts
in one more reviewed file;

65
00:04:21.218 --> 00:04:26.304
to a managed identity or an app
registration, Chapter 5 moves it.

66
00:04:26.304 --> 00:04:30.763
Keep: it stays, with an
end date no later than G 5,

67
00:04:30.763 --> 00:04:33.655
and an S P N owner if it has S P N's.

68
00:04:33.655 --> 00:04:39.259
Keep Kerberos: it stays on Kerberos with
the authorizing official's acceptance,

69
00:04:39.259 --> 00:04:43.505
an S P N owner, and an end date no
later than decision D oh eight's.

70
00:04:43.505 --> 00:04:47.367
Or retire, through the plan's
reviewed decommission process.

71
00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:52.556
The program adds five rules of its own,
its design guidance, not Microsoft's.

72
00:04:52.556 --> 00:04:54.980
The disposition is Chapter 3's.

73
00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.060
Who may retrieve a managed
password is reviewed data,

74
00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.580
not whatever Active Directory holds today.

75
00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:05.093
A D M S A migration runs thirty
days before it completes.

76
00:05:05.093 --> 00:05:08.167
Every account that stays
ends within its bound.

77
00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:11.910
And never delete: the old
account is disabled at most.

78
00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.359
The check runs nine rules, N
P I K R B oh one to oh nine.

79
00:05:20.359 --> 00:05:24.709
Oh one, oh four and oh eight come from
Microsoft Learn's pages on the root key,

80
00:05:24.709 --> 00:05:29.460
delegated managed service accounts and
securing group managed service accounts;

81
00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:32.008
the others are the
program's design guidance.

82
00:05:32.008 --> 00:05:33.714
Oh one: there's no K D S root key,

83
00:05:33.714 --> 00:05:37.162
or one that became usable
less than ten hours ago.

84
00:05:37.162 --> 00:05:40.326
Oh two: a Kerberos
account has no disposition

85
00:05:40.326 --> 00:05:43.204
in Chapter 3's decisions,
or an unknown one.

86
00:05:43.204 --> 00:05:46.518
Oh three: the accounts
allowed to retrieve a managed

87
00:05:46.518 --> 00:05:49.768
password in Active Directory
differ from the reviewed list.

88
00:05:49.768 --> 00:05:52.498
Oh four is the one to
remember: an unusable target.

89
00:05:52.498 --> 00:05:57.459
Learn's overview and F A Q for delegated
managed service accounts say a D M S A

90
00:05:57.459 --> 00:05:59.918
can't come from a managed service account,

91
00:05:59.918 --> 00:06:04.693
can't serve an account with unconstrained
delegation, which stops working once

92
00:06:04.693 --> 00:06:09.613
the migration completes, and needs Windows
Server 2025 on the hosts and the domain.

93
00:06:09.613 --> 00:06:14.344
Oh five and oh six: an account that stays
with no S P N owner where Chapter 3 asks

94
00:06:14.344 --> 00:06:17.489
for one, or no end date,
or one past its bound:

95
00:06:17.489 --> 00:06:21.929
D oh eight's for an account kept
on Kerberos, G 5 for any other.

96
00:06:21.929 --> 00:06:26.000
Oh seven: an account not
reconciled with the end state.

97
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.398
Oh eight warns about a privileged group,
and oh nine hands SQL Server accounts on.

98
00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:39.445
Each account becomes exactly
one action in the plan.

99
00:06:39.445 --> 00:06:41.186
The G M S A path: create it,

100
00:06:41.186 --> 00:06:45.583
once the root key is ready and
the retrievers are reviewed.

101
00:06:45.583 --> 00:06:49.845
Wait while the server owner installs
it and changes the service's identity.

102
00:06:49.845 --> 00:06:53.218
Then a thirty-day parallel
run, the program's guidance,

103
00:06:53.218 --> 00:06:55.595
and only then disable the old account.

104
00:06:55.595 --> 00:07:01.520
The D M S A path: create it, if it passes
every limit, and start the migration.

105
00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.570
After the thirty-day
parallel run, complete it.

106
00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:09.272
Microsoft Learn's overview of
delegated managed service accounts says

107
00:07:09.272 --> 00:07:13.583
completing disables the old account
and moves its S P N's to the D M S A.

108
00:07:13.583 --> 00:07:17.658
Set retrievers fixes a list that
differs from the reviewed one.

109
00:07:17.658 --> 00:07:21.011
Anything else, like island,
blocked or handed off,

110
00:07:21.011 --> 00:07:24.282
is recorded with its
reason, and writes nothing.

111
00:07:24.282 --> 00:07:26.691
The plan carries every input's hash,

112
00:07:26.691 --> 00:07:32.370
and the same inputs always give the same
bytes, so a reviewer approves one hash.

113
00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:37.545
These are the kit's design.

114
00:07:37.545 --> 00:07:40.157
Two outputs carry the chapter's end date.

115
00:07:40.157 --> 00:07:43.956
The island list holds every
account that stays on Kerberos,

116
00:07:43.956 --> 00:07:47.135
with its disposition from
Chapter 3, its S P N owner,

117
00:07:47.135 --> 00:07:49.615
its end date and the date that bounds it,

118
00:07:49.615 --> 00:07:54.189
the authorizing official's acceptance
for an account kept on Kerberos,

119
00:07:54.189 --> 00:07:58.995
and whether it's ready for P four point
six, the program's S P N retirement work.

120
00:07:58.995 --> 00:08:01.982
The hand-off is ready
only when every row is.

121
00:08:01.982 --> 00:08:06.165
The reconciliation covers
every G M S A and D M S A,

122
00:08:06.165 --> 00:08:10.133
existing or planned, and
says where it ends up.

123
00:08:10.133 --> 00:08:13.296
Writing to the domain
lives in a separate script,

124
00:08:13.296 --> 00:08:18.278
and it refuses to run unless the plan's
hash is the approved one, the plan is for

125
00:08:18.278 --> 00:08:21.046
this domain and no more
than seven days old,

126
00:08:21.046 --> 00:08:23.419
and it's a person's session, never a job.

127
00:08:23.419 --> 00:08:27.520
Nothing that removes, undoes
or resets is ever run.

128
00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:30.329
And the read holds no
domain administration:

129
00:08:30.329 --> 00:08:35.567
Microsoft Learn's page for the root key
class says its default security descriptor

130
00:08:35.567 --> 00:08:39.135
grants only Enterprise Admins,
Domain Admins and SYSTEM,

131
00:08:39.135 --> 00:08:44.510
so the read gets a delegated read of the
key's start and create times, nothing more.

132
00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:48.985
Try it.

133
00:08:48.985 --> 00:08:54.660
Lab 1 runs in the non-person identity
folder of the examples, in PowerShell 7.

134
00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:59.917
Its step one makes the three runs Chapter
6 reads: Chapter 1's register, Chapter

135
00:08:59.917 --> 00:09:05.010
2's owners and Chapter 3's decisions, so
the chapter's steps are one ahead here.

136
00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.160
It prints nothing.

137
00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:10.581
The three folders hold the register,
the owners and the owners' D

138
00:09:10.581 --> 00:09:15.172
N P three decisions, which Chapter
6 reads instead of a second list.

139
00:09:15.172 --> 00:09:18.747
Step two runs Microsoft
Learn's published gMSA,

140
00:09:18.747 --> 00:09:24.522
dMSA and root key procedures against the
stand-ins: seventeen pass, none fail.

141
00:09:24.522 --> 00:09:27.160
Step three lists what each task runs.

142
00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:32.326
The read task, kerberos island, runs
only the directory Get cmdlets:

143
00:09:32.326 --> 00:09:37.585
computers, domain controllers,
objects, service accounts and users.

144
00:09:37.585 --> 00:09:42.028
The write task, kerberos apply, adds
the cmdlets that create and set a

145
00:09:42.028 --> 00:09:46.710
service account, start and complete
a migration, and disable an account.

146
00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:51.985
Notice what's missing from both lists: no
cmdlet that removes or undoes anything.

147
00:09:51.985 --> 00:09:55.590
The output on the slide is
trimmed and wrapped to fit.

148
00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.965
Step four checks the Kerberos
accounts and builds the plan.

149
00:10:02.965 --> 00:10:06.365
Nine accounts are in scope,
and the root key is ready.

150
00:10:06.365 --> 00:10:10.402
There are five findings, one
warning and two information items.

151
00:10:10.402 --> 00:10:13.893
The plan has nine actions,
four of them writes:

152
00:10:13.893 --> 00:10:18.891
one completed migration, two new G
M S A's, and one retriever change.

153
00:10:18.891 --> 00:10:20.065
Nothing is sent.

154
00:10:20.065 --> 00:10:22.365
The island list holds six accounts,

155
00:10:22.365 --> 00:10:26.490
four of them ready for P four point
six, so the hand-off isn't ready.

156
00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:30.128
The reconciliation leaves
one account unreconciled.

157
00:10:30.128 --> 00:10:32.717
The dispositions come
from Chapter 3's run,

158
00:10:32.717 --> 00:10:36.565
and the program dates file must
have the hash that run recorded.

159
00:10:36.565 --> 00:10:40.440
The plan's hash is the same on
every run with these inputs.

160
00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.765
Step five reads the findings.

161
00:10:42.765 --> 00:10:46.900
svc webfront is trusted for
unconstrained delegation,

162
00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:50.855
which stops working once a
D M S A migration completes,

163
00:10:50.855 --> 00:10:56.518
so in Chapter 3 its owner kept it on
Kerberos, with the authorizing official's

164
00:10:56.518 --> 00:11:01.102
acceptance; N P I K R B oh five flags
it because it has no S P N owner.

165
00:11:01.102 --> 00:11:07.602
Oh five and oh six flag svc intranet:
Chapter 3 re-platforms it to a G M S A,

166
00:11:07.602 --> 00:11:12.164
but it has no S P N owner and
its end date is after G 5.

167
00:11:12.164 --> 00:11:16.054
The kept G M S A's end in
August twenty twenty-seven,

168
00:11:16.054 --> 00:11:20.040
after D oh eight but before
G 5, which is their bound.

169
00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:23.970
And oh eight warns that gmsa
ops is in Domain Admins.

170
00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:29.432
Step six reads the plan.

171
00:11:29.432 --> 00:11:32.220
Nine actions, and these
are the four writes.

172
00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:34.383
Set retrievers for gmsa web.

173
00:11:34.383 --> 00:11:37.546
Create a G M S A for the managed service

174
00:11:37.546 --> 00:11:41.321
account msa print oh one,
and for svc intranet.

175
00:11:41.321 --> 00:11:45.018
And complete the D M S A
migration for svc h r sync,

176
00:11:45.018 --> 00:11:50.707
whose migration started forty days
ago, past the thirty-day parallel run.

177
00:11:50.707 --> 00:11:56.858
gmsa ops, gmsa web and svc webfront stay
on the island, as Chapter 3 decided.

178
00:11:56.858 --> 00:12:00.496
The other actions are recorded with
their reasons and write nothing.

179
00:12:00.496 --> 00:12:04.220
Step seven reads the island
list and the reconciliation.

180
00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:08.295
Six island rows, each under the
name the account will end up with

181
00:12:08.295 --> 00:12:12.370
and with its disposition, four of
them ready for P four point six.

182
00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:16.964
gmsa intranet has no S P N owner
and ends at the end of 2028,

183
00:12:16.964 --> 00:12:22.358
so it isn't ready, and its
reconciliation outcome is unreconciled.

184
00:12:22.358 --> 00:12:27.477
svc webfront carries the authorizing
official's acceptance from Chapter 3,

185
00:12:27.477 --> 00:12:30.680
so it becomes ready once
it has an S P N owner.

186
00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:37.142
Step eight is the write path's dry run.

187
00:12:37.142 --> 00:12:40.576
You take the plan's hash, which
in a real run a second person

188
00:12:40.576 --> 00:12:44.156
would review and pass to you,
and give it to the action script.

189
00:12:44.156 --> 00:12:48.967
It reports four would-send lines, one
for each write, and sends nothing.

190
00:12:48.967 --> 00:12:53.280
Step nine runs the same command with
the apply switch, against the fake.

191
00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:58.630
Four writes are sent, including the
completed migration for svc h r sync.

192
00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:02.367
Then you pass a hash of zeros,
a hash nobody approved.

193
00:13:02.367 --> 00:13:06.117
The script refuses: the plan's
hash isn't the approved one,

194
00:13:06.117 --> 00:13:08.643
and it won't run a plan nobody reviewed.

195
00:13:08.643 --> 00:13:12.643
Pasted line by line, your shell
carries on after the refusal.

196
00:13:12.643 --> 00:13:17.668
Step ten runs every test: five
hundred thirteen pass, none fail.

197
00:13:17.668 --> 00:13:19.870
Then delete the output folder.

198
00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:28.491
Lab 2 moves to a test Active
Directory domain, never production,

199
00:13:28.491 --> 00:13:34.206
with at least one Windows Server 2025
domain controller, a Windows Server

200
00:13:34.206 --> 00:13:39.358
2025 member server, an existing K D S
root key, and Chapter 1's Lab 2 run.

201
00:13:39.358 --> 00:13:43.082
Make a traditional test account
and a test managed service account.

202
00:13:43.082 --> 00:13:47.507
Grant the read only the root key's
two date attributes, nothing else.

203
00:13:47.507 --> 00:13:49.774
Run the check, then apply the plan as a

204
00:13:49.774 --> 00:13:52.795
person with Domain Admins
or equivalent rights.

205
00:13:52.795 --> 00:13:58.332
Start the D M S A migration, and complete
it after the thirty-day parallel run.

206
00:13:58.332 --> 00:14:03.095
To back it out, step six runs the undo
command from Microsoft Learn's page on

207
00:14:03.095 --> 00:14:06.686
setting up delegated managed
service accounts, by hand,

208
00:14:06.686 --> 00:14:10.433
because the code never runs it,
re-enables the test account,

209
00:14:10.433 --> 00:14:14.571
removes the test objects by hand,
and deletes the output folders.

210
00:14:14.571 --> 00:14:16.720
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

211
00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:20.220
The workbook's Chapter 6
has the exact commands.

212
00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:25.357
The federal requirement.

213
00:14:25.357 --> 00:14:29.498
The Kerberos accounts are the
on-premises part of Gate 5,

214
00:14:29.498 --> 00:14:33.908
and the guide's validate step names
I A five, I A nine and A U two.

215
00:14:33.908 --> 00:14:37.970
These are NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three controls.

216
00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:41.608
The module's table maps each
lab step to its evidence.

217
00:14:41.608 --> 00:14:45.097
For authenticator management
and change control,

218
00:14:45.097 --> 00:14:48.420
the plan, with the register's
